# xargs - run a command with the words of the input as arguments

## SYNOPSIS
    xargs [-0rt] [-n N] [-L N] [-I R] [-s N] [command [argument ...]]

## DESCRIPTION
Reads words from its input and puts them after a command, as many to a
command line as fit, then runs each command line, one after the other.
It is the glue between a command that lists names (find, ls, grep -l)
and a command that should act on them:

    find Work: -name #?.bak | xargs rm

With no command, Echo is used. Words are split at blanks and line ends;
'...' and "..." keep blanks inside a word, and `\\` takes the next
character as it is (as GNU xargs).

The commands run through the Shell, with their input from NIL: and
their output going where xargs's goes, so
`| xargs grep x | wc -l` works.

## OPTIONS
- **-n** N
  at most N words to a command.
- **-L** N
  the words of at most N input lines to a command. **-l** is the same.
- **-I** R
  one command for each input line; R in the arguments is replaced by
  the line (blanks at its start and end left out). **-i** is the same.
- **-s** N
  command lines of at most N bytes. The default is 512, the Shell's own
  line length.
- **-t** show each command (on the error stream) before it runs.
- **-0** words end only at 0 bytes, and quotes are plain characters:
  for the output of `find -print0`, safe with any name.
- **-r** accepted. With no words at all nothing is run, always (GNU runs
  the command once; BSD and this xargs do not, so `rm` never runs with
  no names).
- **-x** accepted and ignored.

## EXAMPLES
    find Work: -name #?.bak | xargs rm
    find Work:Projects -name #?.c | xargs grep -l main
    find RAM: -type f -print0 | xargs -0 ls -l
    ls -1 Work:Pictures | xargs -n 1 echo
    seq 1 5 | xargs -I {} mkdir RAM:dir-{}
    grep -l -r Amiga Work:Docs | xargs -I {} cp {} DH1:Backup

## RETURN CODES
The worst return code of the commands run (0 when all were fine);
10 bad arguments, a word longer than a command line, or a command that
could not be started (xargs then stops); 20 stopped with Ctrl-C.

## NOTES
Each word is passed as the Shell reads it: in quotes when it has a
blank or a `;` `<` `>` `|` `"` in it (or is empty), with `"` written
`\*"` and `\*` written `\*\*` inside. So names with spaces arrive whole.

xargs puts the words *after* the command. When they must go somewhere
else, as the files for cp, which come before its TO, use **-I** (see
the last example).

`xargs ?` shows the usage line.

## SEE ALSO
